 "ref": "Over the life of the note is not the same as per year, and that difference matters. In a sample of notes listed in the S-E-C's complaint, one six-month note promised a thirty-five percent return. If you could reinvest the whole payout at that rate twice a year, you would end the year with about one point eight two times your money: roughly an eighty-two percent annual return. Even without reinvesting, it works out to seventy percent a year. When an investment promises returns like that, on a fixed schedule, ask the simplest question first: where would that much profit come from, every single time?",
